      "s65-c4-profile",
      "Getting to Know the Client",
      "Investment advisers and their representatives (IARs) are fiduciaries. They must always put the client's needs ahead of their own. Broker-dealers and their agents follow FINRA's suitability rules and the SEC's Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI).\n\nBoth IARs and agents must understand a client's personal and financial situation before recommending anything. This is called suitability. Before making a recommendation, consider the client's:\n• Age\n• Time horizon, meaning how long the money can stay invested\n• Liquidity needs\n• Risk tolerance\n• Tax status\n• Current and future financial needs\n• Other investments\n• Investment experience\n\nA client's personal balance sheet lists assets, like savings, a home, and investments, and liabilities, like a mortgage, credit cards, and car loans. A personal income statement shows money coming in, like salary, and money going out, like household bills.\n\nAge. Younger clients can usually take more risk. Older clients should avoid high-risk investments. Small-cap stocks fit younger investors better. High-dividend blue-chip stocks and investment-grade bonds fit older investors who want retirement income.\n\nA rule of thumb for asset allocation: the percent in stocks should equal 100 minus the client's age. A 35-year-old might put 65% in stocks and stock funds and 35% in bonds.\n\nExperience. Experienced clients may be suited for advanced strategies. Complex, high-risk products, like hedge funds, private equity, venture capital, and options, usually do not fit beginners.\n\nOther investments. Know what the client owns, even at other firms. Look for too much in one holding, especially the employer's stock, which creates business risk. Investments that do not fit the client's profile should be sold and the money put into better choices.",
