      "Taxes, Risk, Time, and Values",
      "Tax status. Municipal bonds may fit clients in high tax brackets but usually not those in low brackets. Municipal bonds do not belong in a tax-deferred account like an IRA, and neither do variable annuities. Treasury interest is free from state income tax, which helps high earners in high-tax states. Qualified dividends are taxed at lower rates.\n\nRisk tolerance. Clients must be comfortable with their investments. Younger clients are often more risk-tolerant. For very risk-averse clients, the safest choices are U.S. government and agency debt. Next come investment-grade bonds and dividend-paying blue-chip stocks. Risk tolerance is usually grouped as conservative, moderate, or aggressive.\n\nTime horizon. Money needed in 3 years or less belongs in short-term fixed income. For 5 to 10 years, consider intermediate bonds and lower-risk blue-chip stocks. Save aggressive, higher-risk investments for horizons longer than 10 years.\n\nLiquidity needs. Money set aside for near-term costs or emergencies should go in short-term money market securities.\n\nValues. Some clients do not want to own companies they see as not socially responsible, such as makers of alcohol, tobacco, or firearms. Some avoid companies that harm the environment or clash with their religious values.\n\nBehavioral finance studies mental habits that can hurt investing:\n• Loss aversion: many people fear losses more than they want gains. They may choose only low-risk investments, hold losing stocks too long, and sell winners too soon. People also tend to get more cautious when managing money for someone they care about.\n• Herd mentality: following the crowd into the latest hot investment, like buying crypto because everyone else is. It can lead to illogical choices.\n• Overconfidence bias: believing you know more than others when you do not, which leads to poor decisions.",
